  "slug": "binding-topic-5fe9",
  "summary": "Six recent preprints map how transporters, disordered regions and integrases recognise ligands, ions and partners at defined sites.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how proteins recognise binding partners and permeant ions at defined sites. Nanobody mapping of the bacterial transporter BasC, combining transport assays, crystallography and AlphaFold3, reveals a preference for the intracellular cavity. Enhanced-sampling molecular dynamics resolves the multimodal landscape by which the FCP1 disordered region folds upon binding Rap74. In the serotonin transporter SERT, Na+ binding at the Na2 site is reported to couple sodium to serotonin transport through TM1, TM5 and TM8 interactions, while XPR1 phosphate selectivity is attributed to direct multi-ion competition in the pore. Three divergent solute-binding proteins share a conserved loss of affinity for perfluorinated ligands, with locally tunable sensitivity to partial fluorination. The HIV-1 integrase study extends this view evolutionarily, indicating that drug-resistance mutations shift folding stability and catalytic geometry only marginally.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
